## GPU Broker

This project has a GPU Broker MCP server (`gpu_*` tools) configured when the local broker is reachable at `http://127.0.0.1:8787`. Server instructions and tool schemas remain authoritative.

Use this guidance only where the local `gpu-broker` MCP is callable.

- Treat Broker inspection and coordination as routine infrastructure for GPU-relevant work. A request or accepted plan authorizes owner-scoped coordination once its `profile_id`, or `project_id`, `gpu_count`, and required thresholds are recorded. Reuse that contract; never infer missing inputs.
- Use the normal bare-metal path: `gpu_coordination` → `gpu_claim_profile` or `gpu_claim` → execute → `gpu_release`. A queued or null lease is not permission to run. After a lease is returned, use only `lease.resources[]`: each resource provides `endpoint` (`id`, `host`, `port`, `ssh_user`), `gpus` (`id`, `gpu_uuid`, `gpu_index`), `cuda_visible_devices`, and `commitment`. The Agent may use the project's normal execution path to start or stop the authorized workload there. Broker does not launch or stop it. Bind observed work after startup and release it when finished.
- Reuse a caller-stable `idempotency_key` when retrying a mutation. Endpoints are project-owned (`owner_project_id`): their owner may add one or retire it into `draining`; draining blocks new placement and does not stop a workload.
- Hanhai22 and other Slurm clusters are SchedulerTargets, never raw SSH endpoints. Use the scheduler adapter for owner-controlled submit, status, and cancel. `PENDING` is not a bare-metal lease; Slurm status and `AllocTRES` establish allocation. `access_required` means ask the user to connect the approved VPN; do not automate it.
- Use `gpu_coordination` for Broker state. Do not bypass Broker allocation or freshness/probing through SSH, SQLite, inventory, remote probes, or `nvidia-smi`.
